WEBVTT

NOTE week08-clip2-rollouts
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip has its own caption along the bottom of the frame, so these cues
NOTE are placed near the top to keep the two from overlapping.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.388 line:2 align:center
Now we ask a clinical question:
will this patient die in the ICU?

2
00:00:05.388 --> 00:00:10.612 line:2 align:center
Ethos answers it with no labels,
no fine-tuning, and no extra model,

3
00:00:10.612 --> 00:00:14.950 line:2 align:center
using only the timeline it
was trained to continue.

4
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.367 line:2 align:center
Starting from the ICU transfer,
the model samples the next token,

5
00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:25.000 line:2 align:center
adds it to the timeline, and repeats,
until it writes either discharge or death.

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:32.017 line:2 align:center
Once the context holds 2,048
tokens, the oldest one drops off.

7
00:00:32.217 --> 00:00:36.769 line:2 align:center
Because each token is sampled,
every run tells a different story.

8
00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:41.979 line:2 align:center
Ethos simulates 20 futures for
each patient. In this example,

9
00:00:41.979 --> 00:00:45.483 line:2 align:center
7 of the 20 end in death.

10
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:52.384 line:2 align:center
The estimated risk is 7 out of
20, or 0.35. With 20 rollouts,

11
00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:59.883 line:2 align:center
the estimate can only take 21
values, from 0 to 1 in steps of 0.05.

12
00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:03.927 line:2 align:center
The same simulations
answer other questions too,

13
00:01:03.927 --> 00:01:09.970 line:2 align:center
such as the length of the ICU stay,
by adding up the interval tokens.

14
00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:14.791 line:2 align:center
Ethos reports an AUC of
0.927 for ICU mortality,

15
00:01:14.791 --> 00:01:22.883 line:2 align:center
where 7% of patients died, and
0.921 for hospital mortality.

16
00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:27.985 line:2 align:center
But when few patients die, a high
AUC can still mean many false alarms.

17
00:01:27.985 --> 00:01:30.232 line:2 align:center
In this illustration,

18
00:01:30.232 --> 00:01:35.079 line:2 align:center
flagging 20 of a hundred patients
catches 6 of the 7 deaths,

19
00:01:35.079 --> 00:01:39.417 line:2 align:center
yet 14 of the 20 alarms are false.

20
00:01:39.617 --> 00:01:45.143 line:2 align:center
Read the results with care. The baselines
are numbers reported by other studies,

21
00:01:45.143 --> 00:01:49.686 line:2 align:center
with their own exclusion criteria.
MIMIC comes from one hospital,

22
00:01:49.686 --> 00:01:53.718 line:2 align:center
and dense records lose
early history in the window.

23
00:01:53.718 --> 00:01:57.949 line:2 align:center
Wornow and colleagues found that
most EHR models are tested on tasks

24
00:01:57.949 --> 00:02:02.380 line:2 align:center
that say little about their
value to a health system.
